{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>The second time</b><br>She answered and said, Awake, O north; my beloved shall come. The sense is that my beloved will go to his garden, and because the north and south winds will be stirred and blow upon my garden [and let its spices flow], then he will come." "<b>The third time</b><br>Awake, O north. When the north wind was stirred and the day changed — instead of their blowing in the garden and its spices flowing, the matter was reversed, and the sense is (that Israel) changed their deeds. Then the Shekhinah came to the garden, and the sense is: I have gone up on high, as it is written, [I will go] and return to My place." "And the sense of let him eat of his pleasant fruit is an allusion to the work of the Tabernacle. And the sense is: He will delight in His resting among them, like the delight of one eating pleasant fruit."], :book :song-of-songs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {1 "Reading עלתנ as 'I have gone up'; the word is textually unusual (possibly עליתי or similar). The bracketed [אלכה] is supplied from Hosea 5:15."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra's commentary on the Canticles" "http://beta.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH002030344/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:44:23.471784704Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}